## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to design scalable supply chain and third-party risk controls for real-time data platforms, including attestation, signing, SBOM management, and incident response coordination with external vendors.

## Key Concepts
- Supply chain security and third-party risk management
- Artifact signing with Sigstore/cosign and SBOM generation
- Per-artifact IAM and ephemeral credentials via AWS STS
- Runtime attestation for serverless and container environments
- Incident response playbooks, containment strategies, and vendor communications

## Code Example
```javascript
// Verify artifact signature and SBOM integrity before deployment
async function verifyArtifact(artifactUrl, sbomUrl, expectedDigest) {
  const artifact = await fetch(artifactUrl);
  const artifactDigest = await computeSHA256(artifact);

  if (artifactDigest !== expectedDigest) {
    throw new Error('Artifact integrity check failed');
  }

  const signature = await cosign.verify(artifact, publicKey);
  const sbom = await spdx.parse(fetch(sbomUrl));
  const components = sbom.getVulnerableDependencies();

  return { signature, components, isTrusted: signature.valid };
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Assess ability to secure extension points with practical controls, not theory.

## Key Concepts
- Plugin isolation boundaries
- Manifest signing and attestation
- Runtime sandboxing and host API policy
- CI gates and security tooling
- Incident response with containment and rollback

## Code Example
```javascript
// Example manifest verification
function verifyManifest(manifest, signature, pubKey){
  if(!verifySignature(signature, manifest, pubKey)) return false;
  if(!isAllowedScope(manifest.scope)) return false;
  return true;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you test sandbox isolation in CI/CD and production?
- What telemetry and alarm thresholds would indicate plugin abuse?

## Why This Is Asked

To assess practical control-plane hardening choices under real-world friction, focusing on mobile app token misuse and multi-tenant isolation.

## Key Concepts

- OAuth2/OIDC security best practices (PKCE, short TTLs)
- Token binding and device fingerprinting
- Token rotation and per-tenant scopes
- Token introspection and revocation
- Observability and IR readiness

## Code Example

```javascript
// Pseudo: rotate refresh token on use and bind to device and tenant
function onRefresh(token, deviceId, tenant) {
  if (!isValidDevice(token.bindInfo, deviceId, tenant)) throw new Error('invalid');
  const newToken = issueAccessToken(tenant, token.tenant);
  rotateRefreshToken(token.id);
  return { accessToken: newToken, refreshToken: getRotatedRefresh(token.id) };
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you test the token binding mechanism in CI/CD?
- What metrics indicate token abuse and how would you respond?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to apply multi-tenant isolation, data governance, and IR planning in a Databricks context beyond generic concepts.

## Key Concepts

- Unity Catalog RBAC and object-level permissions
- Tenant-scoped secret scopes and secret management
- Dynamic data masking and access controls for notebooks
- Audit logs and SIEM integration for detection
- Incident response playbooks and metrics

## Code Example

```javascript
// Pseudo policy enforcement in app layer
function authorize(user, dataset) {
  if (!user.tenants.includes(dataset.tenant)) throw new Error("Access denied");
  // additional masking rules could apply here
  return dataset;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you test isolation guarantees across tenants? 
- What metrics and alerts would you instrument to detect leakage or misuse?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests practical application of phishing-resistant MFA and device-based access in admin contexts, plus rollout, exceptions, and measurable outcomes.

## Key Concepts

- phishing-resistant MFA (FIDO2/WebAuthn)
- passwordless SSO
- device posture (MDM/EMM)
- conditional access rules
- centralized logging (SIEM) and phishing simulations

## Code Example

```javascript
// Example simple authorization gate
function allowAccess(user, device) {
  const okUser = user.isAdmin;
  const okDevice = device.isCompliant;
  const okMFA = user.mfaCompletedWithFido2;
  return okUser && okDevice && okMFA;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you handle a lost FIDO key or key compromise?
- What metrics would you track to gauge phishing-resistant MFA effectiveness?
